1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,131
December, nineteen eighty.

2
00:00:02,131 --> 00:00:04,482
A pine forest in Suffolk, England.

3
00:00:04,482 --> 00:00:12,020
Over three nights, American airmen saw lights, a craft, and something that would be called Britain's Roswell.

4
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:14,233
Tonight we put the log on trial.

5
00:00:14,699 --> 00:00:19,325
Because buried in this story are a lighthouse, a star, and a fireball.

6
00:00:19,325 --> 00:00:22,166
And one of them explains almost everything.

7
00:00:22,633 --> 00:00:23,509
A real memo.

8
00:00:23,509 --> 00:00:25,625
A real deputy base commander.

9
00:00:25,625 --> 00:00:29,494
And a tape recording made in the middle of the night.

10
00:00:29,494 --> 00:00:31,100
This is the case file.

11
00:00:31,566 --> 00:00:32,888
Rendlesham Forest.

12
00:00:32,888 --> 00:00:34,651
Evidence against theory.

13
00:00:34,651 --> 00:00:35,533
Let's begin.

14
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,269
Two American air bases, RAF Woodbridge and RAF Bentwaters, sat side by side in the English countryside.

15
00:00:48,269 --> 00:00:53,166
Between them lay the forest, nearly six square miles of pine.

16
00:00:53,633 --> 00:00:59,538
These were working bases of the United States Air Force, at the height of the Cold War.

17
00:00:59,538 --> 00:01:04,900
Nuclear-era tension, strict security, and a lot of bored people on night shift.

18
00:01:05,366 --> 00:01:08,406
Rendlesham is a quiet corner of Suffolk.

19
00:01:08,406 --> 00:01:14,637
Pine plantation, heathland, flat farm fields, and the sea only a few miles beyond.

20
00:01:14,637 --> 00:01:20,033
At night in winter it is very dark, and very easy to misjudge distance.

21
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:28,333
Around three in the morning on the twenty-sixth of December, guards at the east gate saw lights moving down through the trees.

22
00:01:28,333 --> 00:01:30,633
They thought an aircraft had crashed.

23
00:01:31,099 --> 00:01:33,394
Three men went in to investigate.

24
00:01:33,394 --> 00:01:41,388
Colonel Halt's later memo says they found a glowing metallic object with coloured lights, moving through the trees.

25
00:01:41,388 --> 00:01:44,100
Nearby farm animals went into a frenzy.

26
00:01:44,566 --> 00:01:47,217
That is the report, written two weeks later.

27
00:01:47,217 --> 00:01:49,386
What did they write down that night?

28
00:01:49,386 --> 00:01:50,470
Hold that thought.

29
00:01:50,470 --> 00:01:51,133
It matters.

30
00:01:51,599 --> 00:01:57,792
In January nineteen eighty-one, Halt sent his memo to the British Ministry of Defence.

31
00:01:57,792 --> 00:02:00,743
Its title was simply, Unexplained Lights.

32
00:02:00,743 --> 00:02:04,200
Two words that launched forty years of argument.

33
00:02:04,666 --> 00:02:08,106
At about four in the morning, local police were called.

34
00:02:08,106 --> 00:02:11,547
They saw nothing but the distant sweep of a lighthouse.

35
00:02:11,547 --> 00:02:15,299
In daylight they were shown three small marks in the ground.

36
00:02:15,766 --> 00:02:19,932
The police visit matters, because it is the one outside record.

37
00:02:19,932 --> 00:02:22,644
Civilian officers, no stake in the story.

38
00:02:22,644 --> 00:02:28,133
Their notes mention a lighthouse in the distance, and animal scrapes on the ground.

39
00:02:28,599 --> 00:02:31,133
Three shallow impressions in the soil.

40
00:02:31,133 --> 00:02:37,000
Foresters and police thought they were rabbit diggings, with a fresh scrape around them.

41
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:38,066
Real marks, yes.

42
00:02:38,066 --> 00:02:39,400
But marks from what?

43
00:02:39,866 --> 00:02:41,470
Remember that lighthouse.

44
00:02:41,470 --> 00:02:49,300
Orford Ness sits about five miles beyond the forest, and in nineteen eighty it was one of the brightest lights in Britain.

45
00:02:49,766 --> 00:02:54,633
From the east gate the forest runs about a mile east to a farmer's field.

46
00:02:54,633 --> 00:03:02,366
Look further along that same line of sight and you reach the coast, and the lighthouse, five miles beyond the trees.

47
00:03:02,833 --> 00:03:04,114
And one more clue.

48
00:03:04,114 --> 00:03:09,806
At about the same hour, a bright fireball crossed the sky over southern England.

49
00:03:09,806 --> 00:03:11,372
Astronomers logged it.

50
00:03:11,372 --> 00:03:12,724
Many people saw it.

51
00:03:12,724 --> 00:03:16,566
Many astronomers link it to a rocket body re-entering.

52
00:03:17,033 --> 00:03:23,407
To the eye, a fireball like that looks like something coming down, burning, and breaking apart.

53
00:03:23,407 --> 00:03:27,768
Some researchers tie this one to the re-entry of a Soviet launch.

54
00:03:27,768 --> 00:03:32,733
Either way, it ends in a streak that seems to fall straight into a forest.

55
00:03:33,199 --> 00:03:40,956
So at the very start, we already have a real fireball and a real lighthouse, in the right place, at the right time.

56
00:03:40,956 --> 00:03:41,833
Keep a tally.

57
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:43,776
So the tally so far.

58
00:03:43,776 --> 00:03:47,761
Real lights, real witnesses, a real memo, a real tape.

59
00:03:47,761 --> 00:03:53,371
And on the other side, a lighthouse, a fireball, a star, and rabbit scrapes.

60
00:03:53,371 --> 00:03:56,766
Now let's see which side the evidence favours.

61
00:03:57,233 --> 00:04:04,298
Two nights later, Lieutenant Colonel Charles Halt, the deputy base commander, led a patrol into the forest.

62
00:04:04,298 --> 00:04:07,666
He carried a small tape recorder and narrated live.

63
00:04:08,133 --> 00:04:09,291
That tape exists.

64
00:04:09,291 --> 00:04:16,033
On it, Halt and his men describe strange lights in the woods, and their excitement is easy to hear.

65
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:21,741
Halt describes a red, sun-like light, pulsing and shedding glowing particles.

66
00:04:21,741 --> 00:04:24,600
Then it breaks into pieces and disappears.

67
00:04:25,066 --> 00:04:28,983
The red flash, he said, repeated about every five seconds.

68
00:04:28,983 --> 00:04:33,642
The Orford Ness lighthouse flashes at that rate, from that direction.

69
00:04:33,642 --> 00:04:35,533
The match is hard to ignore.

70
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,711
Next, three star-like lights, two to the north and one to the south.

71
00:04:40,711 --> 00:04:43,552
Halt said they moved and sent down beams.

72
00:04:43,552 --> 00:04:47,433
The brightest star in the south, that night, was Sirius.

73
00:04:47,899 --> 00:04:49,768
Then the radiation reading.

74
00:04:49,768 --> 00:04:58,833
Halt's team measured point zero seven milliroentgen in the depressions, against point zero three or four in the surrounding forest.

75
00:04:59,300 --> 00:05:00,613
It sounds alarming.

76
00:05:00,613 --> 00:05:01,235
It isn't.

77
00:05:01,235 --> 00:05:11,466
That is a tiny number, far below any danger level, from a simple military meter, and the spread is so small that tree roots and soil could cause it.

78
00:05:11,933 --> 00:05:17,392
The meter was an ordinary military survey instrument, read by eye from a needle.

79
00:05:17,392 --> 00:05:24,966
The reading was barely above the forest's own background, and a similar little bump was found half a mile away.

80
00:05:25,433 --> 00:05:27,593
Even so, the night felt unreal.

81
00:05:27,593 --> 00:05:29,475
The lights seemed to dance.

82
00:05:29,475 --> 00:05:32,751
And that is the problem with a forest at night.

83
00:05:32,751 --> 00:05:36,166
The human eye is a terrible measuring instrument.

84
00:05:36,633 --> 00:05:40,796
Two men followed one light for about two miles across the fields.

85
00:05:40,796 --> 00:05:47,266
When they reached it, they found it was just a beacon, still flashing at the same distance as before.

86
00:05:47,733 --> 00:05:56,059
One of the men, John Burroughs, said they followed it for about two miles before they could see it was coming from a lighthouse.

87
00:05:56,059 --> 00:05:58,206
His colleague Ed Cabansag agreed.

88
00:05:58,206 --> 00:06:01,133
Only a beacon light, far off in the distance.

89
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:07,277
A single rule of the night: distant lights in darkness are impossible to judge.

90
00:06:07,277 --> 00:06:08,858
Distance, size, speed.

91
00:06:08,858 --> 00:06:12,666
Your brain guesses, and your guess feels like a fact.

92
00:06:13,133 --> 00:06:16,296
There is even a name for one trick of the eye.

93
00:06:16,296 --> 00:06:23,655
Stare at a single point of light in total darkness, and it seems to drift, even when it is perfectly still.

94
00:06:23,655 --> 00:06:25,030
Pilots know it well.

95
00:06:25,030 --> 00:06:27,300
So do people standing in forests.

96
00:06:27,766 --> 00:06:28,909
So the first test.

97
00:06:28,909 --> 00:06:29,607
The lights.

98
00:06:29,607 --> 00:06:31,766
Did the airmen see something real?

99
00:06:32,233 --> 00:06:34,037
Yes, they saw real lights.

100
00:06:34,037 --> 00:06:35,355
Nobody doubts that.

101
00:06:35,355 --> 00:06:43,333
The question is what the lights were, and we already have three natural candidates that match the times and places.

102
00:06:43,800 --> 00:06:45,898
One more candidate was local.

103
00:06:45,898 --> 00:06:53,640
A former American security policeman later claimed he had modified police vehicle lights to prank the base.

104
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:55,593
It was reported by the BBC.

105
00:06:55,593 --> 00:06:59,066
But there is no evidence for those exact nights.

106
00:06:59,533 --> 00:07:01,096
Then came the stories.

107
00:07:01,096 --> 00:07:11,966
Years later, Sergeant Jim Penniston said he touched a craft, a small triangular machine, warm to the touch, covered in symbols he copied into a notebook.

108
00:07:12,433 --> 00:07:13,579
Here is the catch.

109
00:07:13,579 --> 00:07:16,954
The official notes from that night do not mention it.

110
00:07:16,954 --> 00:07:20,392
His first statement doesn't mention touching anything.

111
00:07:20,392 --> 00:07:23,066
The account grew over the following years.

112
00:07:23,533 --> 00:07:26,819
The notebook with the symbols surfaced years later.

113
00:07:26,819 --> 00:07:30,686
No other member of the patrol confirmed he touched anything.

114
00:07:30,686 --> 00:07:35,133
A strong claim needs strong support, and this one had none beside it.

115
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:39,004
This is how memory behaves in all strange cases.

116
00:07:39,004 --> 00:07:43,544
It collects detail, and the detail starts to look like evidence.

117
00:07:43,544 --> 00:07:47,800
In the courtroom of facts it counts as testimony, not proof.

118
00:07:48,266 --> 00:07:50,080
What about the landing site?

119
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:53,320
The marks were rabbit scrapes, said the foresters.

120
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:56,948
Some accounts mention scorched bark and broken branches.

121
00:07:56,948 --> 00:08:02,066
But the police saw rabbit scrapes, and no independent record confirms any burn.

122
00:08:02,533 --> 00:08:05,187
Physical evidence for a craft: none.

123
00:08:05,187 --> 00:08:07,325
Photographs of a craft: none.

124
00:08:07,325 --> 00:08:09,463
Radar track of a craft: none.

125
00:08:09,463 --> 00:08:11,233
Debris of a craft: none.

126
00:08:11,699 --> 00:08:21,066
And the Ministry of Defence, which got the memo, concluded that nothing of defence significance had occurred, so it never launched an investigation.

127
00:08:21,533 --> 00:08:27,302
In twenty ten, Jenny Randles, who wrote the first book on the case, said it plainly.

128
00:08:27,302 --> 00:08:29,294
No unearthly craft were seen.

129
00:08:29,294 --> 00:08:36,299
The main events, she said, were misperceptions of everyday things, in far from everyday circumstances.

130
00:08:36,766 --> 00:08:39,766
So how did a story of lights become a legend?

131
00:08:39,766 --> 00:08:41,166
Follow the retelling.

132
00:08:41,633 --> 00:08:46,918
In October nineteen eighty-three, a British Sunday tabloid ran it as a story.

133
00:08:46,918 --> 00:08:50,899
They called it Britain's Roswell, and a legend had a name.

134
00:08:51,366 --> 00:08:53,643
Books followed, then television.

135
00:08:53,643 --> 00:08:55,778
Each retelling made it bigger.

136
00:08:55,778 --> 00:08:57,557
A craft became a landing.

137
00:08:57,557 --> 00:08:59,478
A landing became a meeting.

138
00:08:59,478 --> 00:09:02,466
A meeting became a message in binary code.

139
00:09:02,933 --> 00:09:07,543
Each stage came from a witness who was sincere, and decades later.

140
00:09:07,543 --> 00:09:08,452
No new facts.

141
00:09:08,452 --> 00:09:09,500
Just new words.

142
00:09:09,966 --> 00:09:12,458
Skeptic Brian Dunning put it neatly.

143
00:09:12,458 --> 00:09:17,789
Separate pieces of poor evidence do not add up to one piece of good evidence.

144
00:09:17,789 --> 00:09:22,566
A fireball, a lighthouse and a rabbit scrape are three small stories.

145
00:09:22,566 --> 00:09:25,266
Told together, they become a spaceship.

146
00:09:25,733 --> 00:09:28,738
Today the forest even has a marked trail.

147
00:09:28,738 --> 00:09:34,748
In two thousand five, the Forestry Commission opened a UFO trail with a sculpture.

148
00:09:34,748 --> 00:09:38,266
It is a gentle walk, and a slightly magical one.

149
00:09:38,733 --> 00:09:40,991
Why does the case still grip us?

150
00:09:40,991 --> 00:09:47,341
Cold war bases, a deputy commander on a tape, and a forest just a short drive from a town.

151
00:09:47,341 --> 00:09:49,670
It has everything a legend needs.

152
00:09:49,670 --> 00:09:53,833
Except the one thing a legend can't borrow, which is proof.

153
00:09:54,299 --> 00:09:56,614
So the detective's question is simple.

154
00:09:56,614 --> 00:09:59,233
Which parts are evidence, which are theory?

155
00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:01,279
First the alien theory.

156
00:10:01,279 --> 00:10:05,399
Visitors landed, left marks, and the military covered it up.

157
00:10:05,866 --> 00:10:08,891
To check it, ask what a landing would leave.

158
00:10:08,891 --> 00:10:09,441
A craft.

159
00:10:09,441 --> 00:10:09,854
Marks.

160
00:10:09,854 --> 00:10:11,297
Damage to the ground.

161
00:10:11,297 --> 00:10:13,566
Something solid the next morning.

162
00:10:14,033 --> 00:10:15,123
There are marks.

163
00:10:15,123 --> 00:10:17,167
They look like rabbit scrapes.

164
00:10:17,167 --> 00:10:21,186
No debris, no confirmed burn, no radiation worth a mention.

165
00:10:21,186 --> 00:10:23,366
The theory needs more than that.

166
00:10:23,833 --> 00:10:25,703
Second, the natural theory.

167
00:10:25,703 --> 00:10:32,766
The lights were a lighthouse, a star, and a fireball, seen through fog by tired men expecting trouble.

168
00:10:33,233 --> 00:10:34,890
Every key detail fits.

169
00:10:34,890 --> 00:10:36,547
The five second pulse.

170
00:10:36,547 --> 00:10:37,677
The south star.

171
00:10:37,677 --> 00:10:39,710
The timing of the fireball.

172
00:10:39,710 --> 00:10:43,100
The beacon two men chased, and never reached.

173
00:10:43,566 --> 00:10:48,053
But that theory has a gap too, and honesty requires us to say so.

174
00:10:48,053 --> 00:10:52,333
It can't explain every claim, and some witnesses still insist.

175
00:10:52,799 --> 00:10:54,911
Third, the secret test theory.

176
00:10:54,911 --> 00:10:57,937
A hush-hush aircraft or a Soviet satellite.

177
00:10:57,937 --> 00:11:03,637
But there is no document, no witness inside the programme, and no sign of either.

178
00:11:03,637 --> 00:11:05,466
A theory without evidence.

179
00:11:05,933 --> 00:11:06,808
And the tape?

180
00:11:06,808 --> 00:11:12,663
Listen to it, and you hear men talking over each other, amazed, laughing, quite unsure.

181
00:11:12,663 --> 00:11:14,614
It is a record of excitement.

182
00:11:14,614 --> 00:11:16,566
It isn't a record of a craft.

183
00:11:17,033 --> 00:11:18,506
Now stack the three.

184
00:11:18,506 --> 00:11:21,009
The fireball explains the descent.

185
00:11:21,009 --> 00:11:24,396
The lighthouse explains the pulsing red light.

186
00:11:24,396 --> 00:11:27,563
A bright star explains the hovering lights.

187
00:11:27,563 --> 00:11:29,772
And rabbits explain the holes.

188
00:11:29,772 --> 00:11:33,233
Four ordinary answers for four strange details.

189
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:35,878
What could change the verdict?

190
00:11:35,878 --> 00:11:36,821
A photograph.

191
00:11:36,821 --> 00:11:37,910
A radar return.

192
00:11:37,910 --> 00:11:39,217
A piece of debris.

193
00:11:39,217 --> 00:11:42,121
A document from inside a real programme.

194
00:11:42,121 --> 00:11:44,880
Any one of them would reopen the case.

195
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:47,566
After forty years, none has appeared.

196
00:11:48,033 --> 00:11:49,519
Time to score the case.

197
00:11:49,519 --> 00:11:52,233
Evidence on one side, theory on the other.

198
00:11:52,700 --> 00:11:56,625
Real airmen saw real lights on real nights: evidence.

199
00:11:56,625 --> 00:12:00,033
A memo and a tape from Colonel Halt: evidence.

200
00:12:00,033 --> 00:12:04,700
A fireball and a lighthouse that match the sightings: evidence.

201
00:12:05,166 --> 00:12:08,448
Touching a craft, years later: unverified.

202
00:12:08,448 --> 00:12:10,402
Radiation: tiny, partial.

203
00:12:10,402 --> 00:12:13,450
Landing marks: rabbit diggings, likely.

204
00:12:13,450 --> 00:12:16,966
A craft, a crash, or a cover-up: no evidence.

205
00:12:17,433 --> 00:12:18,282
The verdict.

206
00:12:18,282 --> 00:12:19,273
Misperception.

207
00:12:19,273 --> 00:12:24,087
Rendlesham is a real event, with real witnesses who saw real lights.

208
00:12:24,087 --> 00:12:31,166
But the lights were most likely a lighthouse, a star and a fireball, and the legend grew from there.

209
00:12:31,633 --> 00:12:33,667
That doesn't make the men liars.

210
00:12:33,667 --> 00:12:38,500
It makes them human, in the dark, in the cold, in a story that kept growing.

211
00:12:38,966 --> 00:12:44,087
What lingers is the mood of that forest, three winter nights and a lot of stars.

212
00:12:44,087 --> 00:12:47,799
And a question about how much we should trust what we see.

213
00:12:48,266 --> 00:12:54,933
Next case: a signal from space, seventy-two seconds long, and a note written in red pen on a printout.

214
00:12:55,399 --> 00:12:57,402
What do you think the airmen saw?

215
00:12:57,402 --> 00:13:02,500
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
